# cmus configuration — catppuccin frappe, matching kitty and tmux.
#
# Why this file and not a .theme: cmus reads three files in order — autosave,
# the system rc, then this one — and this is the only one it never writes back
# (`man cmus`, FILES). Colours in particular are *saved into autosave on exit*,
# which the man page warns about under Color Schemes: a .theme applies once,
# then autosave carries whatever was current and a later edit to the theme does
# nothing. Setting them here means they are re-applied at every start and
# autosave cannot drift away from them.
#
# That is the same two-writer shape as ~/.claude/settings.json and karabiner.json
# (see CLAUDE.md), except cmus hands us a file it promises not to touch — so no
# `chezmoi re-add` dance is needed here.

# ── Colours ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cmus takes a colour as an integer -1..255, not a hex value. Two consequences
# shaped everything below.
#
# -1 is "the terminal's own colour", and 0-15 are the terminal's ANSI palette —
# which kitty has set to frappe (dot_config/kitty/catppuccin-frappe.conf). So a
# named colour here resolves to the *exact* frappe value, with no approximation
# at all:
#
#     default      -1   base  #303446 / text #c6d0f5   (terminal bg/fg)
#     black         0   surface1 #51576d
#     darkgray      8   surface2 #626880
#     gray          7   subtext1 #b5bfe2
#     blue          4   blue  #8caaee      magenta  5  pink  #f4b8e4
#     cyan          6   teal  #81c8be      yellow   3  yellow #e5c890
#     green         2   green #a6d189      red      1  red   #e78284
#
# The 256-colour cube was the alternative and it is measurably worse: frappe's
# backgrounds are blue-tinted greys the cube cannot express, so base #303446
# lands on #3a3a3a — a flat grey, 17 units away, sitting next to a terminal
# painting the real thing. Names it is.
#
# Nothing here uses a light background with a dark foreground, because the ANSI
# sixteen carry no colour dark enough to read against blue or pink. Selections
# are surface1 with a coloured foreground instead, which is how a dark theme
# wants to be read anyway.

set color_win_bg=default
set color_win_fg=default
set color_win_dir=blue
set color_win_cur=magenta

set color_win_sel_bg=black
set color_win_sel_fg=default
set color_win_cur_sel_bg=black
set color_win_cur_sel_fg=magenta
set color_win_inactive_sel_bg=black
set color_win_inactive_sel_fg=gray
set color_win_inactive_cur_sel_bg=black
set color_win_inactive_cur_sel_fg=gray

set color_win_title_bg=black
set color_win_title_fg=default
set color_titleline_bg=black
set color_titleline_fg=blue

set color_statusline_bg=black
set color_statusline_fg=default
set color_statusline_progress_bg=black
set color_statusline_progress_fg=green

set color_cmdline_bg=default
set color_cmdline_fg=default
set color_error=red
set color_info=yellow
set color_separator=darkgray
set color_trackwin_album_bg=default
set color_trackwin_album_fg=cyan
